Using the bounds in \eqref{gamma quotient bounds} the integral in \eqref{Dq integral} along the component boundary, before multiplying by factor $T^m$, is bounded by
\begin{equation}\label{component bound}
C_{n,L,a} T^{C_n Ma}U^{C_n Ma}R_{j,k,q},
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation*}             
R_{j,k,q}=T^{nm_j-nk} U_j^{-nm_j/2+nk-m/2-1/2+q} e^{-\pi d_j/2}\prod_{i=1}^{n}d_{ij}^{(m_j-m_i-1)/2-k}.
\end{equation*}
\par 
We spell out the loss in \eqref{component bound}. Perturbating $\sigma$ by $O(Ma)$ changes $T^{n\sigma}$ by at most $T^{C_n Ma}$. It changes the exponents of the polynomials in \eqref{gamma quotient bounds} by $O_n(Ma)$, producing at most $U^{C_n Ma}$. Bounded perturbations of the imaginary argument change $1+|t|$ and $1+|t-{\tau}_i|$ by bounded ratios. Finally for the exponential, we use $\sum_{j=1}^{n}|t-{\tau}_j|-n|t|\geq 2\min(|{\tau}_1-t|,|t-{\tau}_n|),$ for ${\tau}_n\leq t\leq {\tau}_1$, and $0$ outside this region and also the fact that $\mathcal{E}(t)$ is Lipschitz   with constant at most $2n$ so its exponential changes only by a bounded factor. These observations prove \eqref{component bound}, including at multiple poles.
\\\\
\textbf{Reduction to $R_{j,0,q}$.} The ratio between $R_{j,k,q}$ and $R_{j,0,q}$ is 
\begin{equation*}
   \left(\frac{U_j}{T}\right)^{nk}\prod_{i=1}^{n}d_{ij}^{-k}.
\end{equation*}
Since $U_j\leq U\leq C T^{2-{\kappa}}$, this ratio is bounded by a constant depending only on $n,L,C$. Therefore it suffices to estimate $R_{j,0,q}$. This argument is valid even if a pole that formally appears in \eqref{poles} is canceled: we are only bounding the corresponding contour integral.
\\\\
\textbf{Replacement of the real part by its maximum.} In the explicit expression of $R_{j,0,q}$, we form $R'_{j,0,k}$, by replacing the occurrences of $m_j$ outside the list $m_1,...,m_n$ by ${\beta}=\|\Re \mu\|_\infty$, while keeping each $m_i$ in that list fixed, i.e.
\begin{equation}\label{R'}
    R'_{j,0,q}=T^{n\beta} U_j^{-n\beta/2-m/2-1/2+q} 
    e^{-\pi d_j/2}\prod_{i=1}^{n}d_{ij}^{(\beta-m_i-1)/2}.
\end{equation}
The ratio of the original expression to the new one is 
\begin{equation*}
\frac{R_{j,0,q}}{R'_{j,0,q}}=T^{n(m_j-{\beta})}U_j^{-n(m_j-{\beta})/2}\prod_{i=1}^{n}d_{ij}^{\frac{m_j-{\beta}}{2}}\leq \left(\frac{U_j}{T^2}\right)^{n({\beta}-m_j)/2}\ll_{n,C} 1,
\end{equation*}
where we used that $m_j\leq {\beta}$, hence every factor in the product is at most $1$ and then used $U_j\leq C T^{2-{\kappa}}$. This provides the precise comparison behind the monotonicity in the real part used in \cite[p.10]{blomerlutsko}.
\\\\
\textbf{Removal of the interior imaginary parts.} If $U$ is bounded, all the remaining spectral factors are bounded above and below by constants depending only on $n$ and there is nothing further to prove. Suppose that $U$ is large. Since ${\tau}_1\asymp -{\tau}_n\asymp \|{\tau}\|_\infty\asymp_{n} U$, then either $U\asymp U_j$, or $d_j\geq cU $ for a fixed $c>0$ depending only on $n$. In the latter case $e^{-\pi d_j /2}$ absorbs every fixed power of $U$. All other spectral factors have exponents bounded in terms of $n$; moreover, with $\mathcal{G}({\tau})$ as in \eqref{G definition} 
\begin{equation*}                
\mathcal{G}({\tau})\gg_n U^{-(n-1)/2}.
\end{equation*}
Indeed, each factor in the products of $\eqref{G definition}$, without the power, are smaller or equal than a quantity which is $\asymp U$, the negative power switches the inequality and there are $n-1$ factors in the product.
 \par
 Each term in the product of \eqref{R'} is $\ll U$ and the exponents are bounded by $(\beta-1)/2$. There are $n-1$ factors in the sum contributing, since the $i=j$ term is equal to $1$, therefore the whole product is $\ll_n U^{(n-1)(\beta-1)/2}$ and in this case
 \begin{equation*}
R'_{j,0,q}\ll T^{n\beta}U^{q-m/2-(1+\beta)/2}\mathcal{G}({\tau}),
 \end{equation*}
satisfying the desired estimate.
We may henceforth assume $U_j\asymp_n U$.
\\\\
\textbf{The use of unitarity.} After replacement by $\beta$ all numbers $\beta-m_i$ are non negative and 
\begin{equation*}
    \sum_{i=1}^{n}\frac{{\beta}-m_i}{2}=\frac{n{\beta} }{2}.
\end{equation*}
There is an index $i_0$ such that 
\begin{equation}\label{i_0 imaginary}
{\tau}_{i_0}={\tau}_j, \quad m_{i_0}\leq 0.
\end{equation}
Indeed, if $m_j\leq 0$ take $i_0=j.$ If $m_j>0$, the unitary symmetry  supplies the coordinate $-\overline{{\mu}_j}=-m_j+i{\tau}_j$. For this index $d_{i_0 ,j}=1$; hence the exponent $({\beta}-m_{i_0})/2$, which is at least $\beta/2$, contributes no power of $U$. All remaining $n-1$ factors satisfy $d_{ij}=O_n(U)$. It follows that 
\begin{equation}\label{endpoints 1}
\prod_{i=1}^{n}d_{ij}^{\frac{\beta-m_i}{2}}\ll_n U^{n\beta/2-\beta/2}. 
\end{equation}
Dividing this estimate by $U_j^{n\beta/2}$ leaves precisely the factor $U^{-\beta/2}$. This argument explains why the unitary symmetry, and not just a bounded real strip, is needed for the stated sharp estimate. It also proves the estimate when some or all imaginary coordinates coincide.
\\\\
\textbf{Reduction to the endpoint products.} Choose the closest endpoint $\psi\in\{{\tau}_1,{\tau}_n\}$ to ${\tau}_j$. Then $|\psi-{\tau}_j|=d_j$, and for every $i$ 
\begin{equation*}
    1+|\psi-{\tau}_i|\leq(1+d_j)(1+|{\tau}_j-{\tau}_i|).
\end{equation*}
Thus 
\begin{equation}\label{endpoints 2}
    e^{-\pi d_j/2}\prod_{i=1}^{n}d_{ij}^{-1/2}\leq e^{-\pi d_j/2}(1+d_j)^{n/2}\prod_{i=1}^{n}(1+|\psi-{\tau}_i|)^{-1/2}\ll_{n}\mathcal{G}({\tau}),
\end{equation}
where the exponential kills the polynomial growth in $d_j$ and the remaining product is either one of the products of $\mathcal{G}(\tau)$ depending on which value $\psi$ takes.
Combining \eqref{endpoints 1} and \eqref{endpoints 2} and recalling $U\asymp_n U_j$, we get
\begin{equation}\label{endpoints 3}
    R_{j,k,q}\ll_{n,L,C}T^{n\beta} U^{q-m/2-(1+\beta)/2} \mathcal{G}({\tau}).
\end{equation}
\\
\textbf{Completion of the contour estimate.} There are at most $M=M(n,L)$ components. Fix their radius $a$ so small so that the product $T^{C_n Ma}U^{C_n Ma}$ is $O(T^{\eta})$, for some $\eta>0$ and small. This is possible, since $U\ll T^2$. Multiply \eqref{endpoints 3} by the outside factor $T^m$ and sum over the components. The contribution of the far left line can be made smaller than any fixed negative power of $T$, by \eqref{far left 1} and \eqref{far left 2}. It can therefore be absorbed in \eqref{Dq estimate}: using ${\beta}\leq (n-1)/2$,  and $U\ll T^2$ the right hand side of \eqref{Dq estimate} is bounded below by some negative power of $T$. This completes the proof.

\label{ball estimates}
For every $\eta>0$, as $T\to\infty$,
\begin{equation}  \label{volume of ball bound}
\hbox{vol}(B_T) = a_nT^d+O_{n,\eta}(T^{d-n+\eta}),  \end{equation}
and 
\begin{equation} \label{volume of D ball bound}
D\hbox{vol}(B_T) = m \, a_nT^d+O_{n,\eta}(T^{d-n+\eta}),
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation} \label{leading term coefficient}
a_n = \frac{\pi^{\frac{m}{2}}}{2^{n-1}\Gamma(m+1)}\prod_{j=1}^{n-1}\Gamma(j/2).
\end{equation}
In particular
\begin{equation}\label{main term asymptotics}
    M_0(T)=M_n(T)+O_{n,\eta}(T^{d-n+\eta})
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation} \label{main term asymptotics2}
D \, M_0(T)= m \, c_nT^d+O_{n,\eta}(T^{d-n+\eta}),
\end{equation}
with $c_n=a_n/V$. Moreover, for $0<\delta\leq1/2$ and sufficiently large $T$,
\begin{equation}\label{main term approximation}
|M_0(T(1+\delta)^{\pm 1})-M_0 (T)|\ll_n\delta T^d.
\end{equation}

The constant spherical function satisfies $\phi_\rho (g)=1$. Using the spherical transform  \eqref{spherical 1 step} and $h_T$ as in Proposition \ref{new transform bound}  then $h_{T}(\rho)=\hbox{vol}(B_T)$. Applying \eqref{spherical} with ${\mu}=\rho$ we get 
    \begin{equation}\label{mellin for constant term}
        h_T(\rho)=\frac{\pi^{\frac{m}{2}}}{2^{n}}T^m\int_{(c)}T^{ns}\frac{\prod_{j=1}^n\Gamma((s-\rho_j)/2)}{\Gamma(ns/2+m/2+1)}\,\frac{ds}{2\pi i},
    \end{equation}
    with $\rho_j=\frac{n-2j+1}{2}$.
    The possible poles are 
    \begin{equation*}
        p_{j,k}=\rho_j-2k,
    \end{equation*}
    and the rightmost is $s_0=(n-1)/2$.
    This is a simple pole, since only the first numerator factor has a pole there and the denominator is nonzero. The residue of $\Gamma((s-s_0)/2)$ is $2$. For the other factors,
    \begin{equation*}
        \frac{s_0-\rho_j}{2}=\frac{j-1}{2},\, (2\leq j\leq n),\quad \frac{ns_0}{2}+\frac{m}{2}+1=m+1.
    \end{equation*}
    The outside and inside powers of $T$ combine to give $T^{m+ns_0}=T^{m+n(n-1)/2}=T^d$. Hence the residue in \eqref{mellin for constant term}  is exactly $a_n T^d$, with $a_n$ as in \eqref{leading term coefficient}.
    The next possible numerator pole is at $s_0-1=(n-3)/2$. Choose a line just to its right, say at $\Re s=s_0-1+2\eta/n$; if necessary first replace $\eta$ by a smaller positive number to keep this line below $s_0$. The contour shift crosses only the rightmost pole and the new integral is absolutely convergent by \eqref{gamma quotient bounds}. Its power of $T$ is 
    \begin{equation*}
        m+n(s_0-1+2\eta/n)=d-n+\eta.
    \end{equation*}
    This proves \eqref{volume of ball bound}. Apply the same argument to \eqref{Dq integral} with $q=1$. Its denominator at the leading pole is $\Gamma(m)$ instead of $\Gamma(m+1)$ multiplying the residue by $m$. The remaining integral has the same power of $T$ proving \eqref{volume of D ball bound}. Dividing the two asymptotics by $V$ proves \eqref{main term asymptotics}. Finally \eqref{volume of D ball bound} gives $DM_0(Y)\ll_{n} Y^{d}$ for large $Y$. Integrating with respect to $dY/Y$ between $T$ and $T(1+\delta)^{\pm1}$ proves \eqref{main term approximation}.

\label{coarse bounds}
Uniformly for parameters satisfying \eqref{spectral parameter conditions} and for sufficiently large $T$
\begin{equation*}
    |h_T({\mu})|\leq \hbox{vol}(B_T)\ll_n T^d,\quad |Dh_T({\mu})|\leq D\hbox{vol}(B_T)\ll_n T^d.
\end{equation*}
The estimates have no restrictions on $U$.

On the unitary spherical spectrum we have $|\phi_{-{\mu}}(g)|\leq 1$. Integration over the ball proves the first inequality. For $Y>T$ the nesting of the balls gives 
  \begin{equation*}
      |h_Y({\mu})-h_T({\mu})|\leq \int_{B_Y\backslash B_T}|\phi_{-{\mu}}(g)|\,dg\leq \hbox{vol}(B_Y)-\hbox{vol}(B_T).
  \end{equation*}
  The same inequality, with the endpoints reversed holds for $Y<T$. Continuous differentiability as function of the radius was established through dominated convergence in the proof of Proposition \ref{new transform bound}. Dividing by $|Y-T|$ and passing to the limit gives $|\partial_Th_T({\mu})|\leq \partial _T\hbox{vol}(B_T)$. Multiplication by $T$ and Lemma \ref{ball estimates} proves the second estimate. In particular, no separate smooth-boundary assertion for the Frobenius ball is needed.

\label{spectral cancellation}
   Uniformly for all real ${\tau}$, with $\sum_{j}{\tau}_j=0$, including the walls of Weyl chambers, we have the bound
    \begin{equation}\label{cancellation in spectral}
        \mathcal{G}^2(\tau) \mathcal{W}(\tau)\ll_n U^{m-r}.
    \end{equation}

The elementary inequality $(a+b)^2\leq 2a^2+2b^2$ gives
    \begin{equation*}
        \mathcal{G}^2\mathcal{W}\leq 2\frac{\mathcal{W}}{P_+}+2\frac{\mathcal{W}}{P_-}.
    \end{equation*}
    Each quotient cancels exactly $r=n-1$ of the $m$ root factors in $\mathcal{W}$. Every remaining factor is at most $2U$ and there are $m-r$ of them. This proves 
\eqref{cancellation in spectral}. The use of $1+|{\tau}_i-{\tau}_j|$ rather than a homogeneous power of the differences is what makes the proof valid at collisions.

\label{maximal estimate}
For every $a\in(0,2)$ and every $\eta>0$
\begin{equation*}
    \left\|\sup_{0\leq t\leq 1}|E_{Q}^{\pm}(t,\cdot)|\right\|_{L^2(X)}\ll_{w,n,a,\eta}Q^{b+\eta}(1+\log Q)
\end{equation*}
with $b=d-\frac{n}{2}$, for both choices of sign.

\label{sobolev inequality}
    If $f\in H^1([0,1];{\mathbb C}),$ with its absolutely continuous representative, then 
    \begin{equation}\label{sobolev 1}
        \sup_{0\leq t\leq 1}|f(t)|^2\leq \int_{0}^{1}|f(t)|^2\,dt+2\left(\int_{0}^{1}|f(t)|^2\,dt\right)^{1/2}\left(\int_{0}^{1}|f'(t)|^2\,dt\right)^{1/2}.
    \end{equation}
Consequently if $F,\partial_tF\in L^2([0,1]\times X)$, then 
    \begin{equation}\label{sobolev 2}
        \left\|\sup_{0\leq t\leq 1}|F(t,\cdot)|\right\|_2^2\leq A_0+2\sqrt{A_0A_1},
        \end{equation}
with        \begin{equation*}A_q=\int_{0}^{1}\|\partial_t^qF(t,\cdot)\|_2^2\,dt,
    \end{equation*}
    where the supremum uses the absolutely continuous representative for almost every point in $X$.

There is a $t_0\in[0,1]$ such that $|f(t_0)|^2\leq \int_{0}^{1}|f|^2$ (otherwise the integral would be strictly smaller than every value of its continuous integrand). Applying the fundamental theorem of calculus and the identity $(|f|^2)'=2\Re(f'\bar{f})$, imply that for every $t\in[0,1]$:
    \begin{equation*}
        |f(t)|^2\leq |f(t_0)|^2+2\int_{0}^{1}|f'(s)|\,|f(s)|\,ds.
    \end{equation*}
A straightforward application of Cauchy-Schwarz proves \eqref{sobolev 1}. The same proof applies to $H^1$ functions because they have absolutely continuous representatives and satisfy the fundamental theorem of calculus.

Fubini's theorem for Sobolev functions gives, for almost every $z\in X$, an $H^1$ function $t\to F(t,z)$ with the indicated weak derivative. Applying \eqref{sobolev 1}, integrating in $z$ and using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality in the $z-$integral for the product of the two square roots, we derive the proof of \eqref{sobolev 2}.

For $z= I_n = w$ we have 
\begin{equation*}
N_n (T;I_n, I_n)  = \#\left\{ \gamma  \in \hbox{SL}_n (\mathbb{Z}) : \| \gamma \|_F \leq T \right\}.
\end{equation*}
If we denote $\gamma = (\gamma_{ij})$ then 
\begin{equation*}
\|\gamma\|_F^2 = \sum_{i,j} \gamma_{i,j}^2 \in \mathbb{Z}. 
\end{equation*}
Hence, when $T^2$ stays between two consecutive integers $m$ and $m+1$, the function $N_n(T; I_n, I_n)$ is constant. Write 
\begin{equation*}
T_1 = \sqrt{m+\frac{1}{4}}, \ T_2 = \sqrt{m+\frac{3}{4}}.
\end{equation*}
Since $N_n (T_1; I_n, I_n) = N_n (T_2; I_n, I_n)$, using the mean value theorem, by \eqref{sharperror} for some $T \in [T_1, T_2]$ we get
\begin{equation*} 
E_n(T_1; I_n, I_n) - E_n (T_2, I_n, I_n) = c_n \left(T_2^{n(n-1)} - T_1^{n(n-1)}\right) \asymp  T ^{n(n-1)-2}.
\end{equation*}
Hence 
\begin{equation*} 
\max \left\{|E_n(T_2; I_n, I_n)|, |E_n (T_1, I_n, I_n)| \right\} \gg  T^{n(n-1)-2},
\end{equation*}
and the desired result follows.
